<!-- PinnedHeader: a heading (and the controls that act on the list below it)
     that stays put while the body scrolls under it. The one way a tab pins
     a header.

     The pattern is the full-height layout, not a sticky: the App has
     scrollWholePage="false" (Main.xmlui; Jon, 2026-11-24: "rejected appwide
     switch: that was my call, maybe a bad one, let's try it"), so the page
     never scrolls, each tab is a bounded column, the header is a plain
     sibling at the top, and the body is the one capping scroller
     (height="*" with overflowY="auto"). StickySection was the first
     attempt: it pins only against its nearest overflow ancestor, which
     inside a splitter pane (overflow:auto in XMLUI's own CSS) or a bounded
     box is not the element that scrolls, so Context and Files never pinned
     while Issues, straight in the page scroller, did. StickyBox is not
     used (410d069f, e60fd6cf).

     Contract for the caller: each route uses Page height="*" minHeight="0".
     Page defaults to min-height:100% and otherwise grows with its content,
     even when its child has height="*". The tab root is a height="*" column
     (or height="100%" directly inside a bounded splitter pane);
     without it the body has nothing to fill and the whole tab grows
     instead. Virtualized Lists and Trees in the body either take the body's
     scroller (an unbounded List defers to it) or their own bounded box.

     Props:
       headerTemplate  the pinned markup. Slot content evaluates in the
               caller's scope, so filter and sort controls in it drive the
               caller's var. state as they did inline (the Issues filters
               prove it).
       children  the scrolling body. Caveat, found 2026-11-24 on
               ContextStack: an emitEvent written in slot content fires in
               this component's scope, not the caller's, so the caller's
               handler never runs. Set a caller var from the slot instead
               and emit from a ChangeListener in the caller's own markup.

     Fragment root, so the caller's own gap applies between the header and
     the body; the component adds no box of its own around the header.
     https://www.xmlui.org/docs/howto/build-a-full-height-scroll-layout
     https://www.xmlui.org/docs/howto/pin-a-toolbar-above-a-bounded-scroll-region
     https://www.xmlui.org/docs/howto/pass-a-template-slot-to-a-component -->
<Component name="PinnedHeader">
  <Fragment>
    <Slot name="headerTemplate" />
    <VStack
      automationId="pinned-body"
      height="*"
      overflowY="auto"
      gap="{$props.gap ?? '$space-4'}">
      <Slot />
    </VStack>
  </Fragment>
</Component>
